assassin's creed has long experimented  with its protagonists having magic-like  abilities oftentimes explored in  post-launch expansions dawn of ragnarok  is the latest adding to assassin's creed  valhalla by pivoting from avor to delve  into the story of javi the acer who is  most well known as odin  dawn of ragnarok doesn't quite reach the  highs found in assassin's creed's  previous myth and legends focused  expansions falling short in both  delivering a compelling narrative and  fully embracing its initial open-ended  gameplay loop however donna ragnarok  does satisfyingly evolve valhalla's  combat and navigation via rewarding  assortment of cool mythical powers  in donna ragnarok javi travels to the  realm of the dwarves to save his son  from suter the flaming isu warlord of  muspelheim sorcerer has invaded with an  army composed of giants from both the  icy ottenheim and fiery moose behind for  some unknown purpose though javi has no  interest in learning what that purpose  is he still finds himself aiding the  besieged dwarves in order to gather the  necessary support to save his son listen  harvey  i understand  this isn't your war  you just want your son back but those  spells on our doorstep  they ain't no joke  if they find us  we're dead meat the campaign attempts to  navigate the complexities of javi's  abusive nature so far we've only seen a  self-proclaimed god who's so  self-assured that he can't find fault in  any of his actions reasoning that his  mistreatment of his allies is a  necessary evil for bettering asgard and  ensuring his own survival for all time  even through the events of ragnarok and  his predetermined demise don of ragnarok  sees javi come face to face with this  aspect of himself and the isu is  regularly presented with examples of how  the ends don't always justify the means  it's an intriguing hook but not one that  gets a satisfying payoff his story just  sort of ends instead the final moments  of the expansion explore evor positing  that the viking learns a lesson that  javi could not and that would be an  interesting development if valhalla's  base campaign hadn't already concluded  with a similar finale so in the end dawn  of ragnarok's campaign feels a lot like  the stories told in wrath of the druids  and the siege of paris and that it  doesn't explore narrative concepts  beyond what's already touched upon in  valhalla it instead retreads old ground  and feels a bit samey as a result in  terms of gameplay however don of  ragnarok introduces several new ideas  for valhalla's formula transforming how  you're able to navigate its world and  fight enemies within it early into his  journey javi is gifted the hygge rip a  gauntlet that allows him to rip the  hooger the mind and emotions of a  creature's soul from dead enemies and  animals and inherit their forms and  abilities each of the powers supports  one of assassin's creed's core tenets  parkour combat and social stealth  creating engaging opportunities to  experiment with how javi can tackle a  problem  thanks hobby i knew you wouldn't let us  down  javi plays a lot like assassin's creed  odyssey's cassandra incorporating a  combination of standard movements and  god-like powers if you're willing to  engage with both move sets and carefully  consider how to use each to your  advantage you can tear through  encampments with an enjoyable ease and a  fulfilling display of strength javi can  only hold onto two huger at a time and  each ability needs to be charged before  it can be used encouraging you to be  strategic in both what you choose to  have access to and when you decide to  utilize it this additional consideration  can lead to moments of rewarding  adaptation but it too easily limits the  experience it's annoying to get to  points in the story where you need a  certain power to proceed but you don't  have it equipped forcing you to  backtrack and find an enemy with the  hooker you need it fortunately doesn't  happen too often but it occurs just  enough to be noticeably irritating the  hygge rip isn't the only big change the  town of ragnarok implements the  expansion opts for a more open-ended  approach to exploration in comparison to  previous assassin's creed games after a  prologue javi emerges from a cave to  find himself a tafa cliff presenting the  player with a breathtaking view of  towering mountains green valleys and  rolling hills the game provides no  waypoint at this moment only loose  directions to find a way to save javi's  son and if time allows discover the  hidden hideaways of the dwarves and aid  them in their fight against suitor that  moment emulates a scene that's become  increasingly familiar in games in the  years following the release of 2017's  the legend of zelda breath of the wild  dawn of ragnarok does initially embody  those clear inspirations kicking off  with a hands-off open world structure  you do get waypoints for your next  mission once you discover a new  narrative thread but completing that  thread leaves you once again devoid of  any waypoint to chase after and so in  donna ragnarok's early hours progression  is solely tied to your freedom to  explore and discover a first for an  assassin's creed game it's refreshingly  novel for the series and there are a few  interesting things to find in those  early hours to keep you excited as what  may hide beyond the next hill however  don of ragnarok reverts to assassin's  creed's traditional structure about  midway through once the story gets going  the expansion starts regularly supplying  you with waypoints including to  locations you may not have had the  pleasure of discovering on your own yet  this keeps the story of the campaign  moving at a nice brisk pace but it  undermines the sense of wonder and  exploration the expansion cultivates in  its early hours and once that happens  the map becomes a place of  interconnected destinations not a space  to explore by the time i finished dawn  of ragnarok i was playing the game by  climbing on my mount telling it to  follow the road to the next waypoint and  pulling up my phone to do a quick social  media doom scroll while waiting for javi  to reach his destination on his own  that's a typical approach for getting  around valhalla but a disappointing  development given the initial promise of  freedom the dawn of ragnarok's early  hours provided dawn of ragnarok is  almost transformative for assassin's  creed teasing the possibility of what  the series could become without the  overt hand-holding that has defined it  for almost two decades however after an  intriguing opening donna ragnarok falls  back on old habits and that feeling  coupled with how the expansion's  narrative themes feel too similar to the  base game creates an experience that too  often resembles something we've already  had before but donna ragnarok is built  on the bones of a great action-adventure  rpg and that carries much of the  expansion especially with the rewarding  considerations introduced with the hygge  rip only time will tell if avor and  hobby's story continues beyond donna  ragnarok but if this is the end it's a  fine enough conclusion to the  relationship between the two  [Music]  let's stretch them  huh
 

